","lem-base-change-affine-morphisms","def-diagonal-morphism-scheme","lem-diagonal-base-change-identification","def-graph-morphism-over-base","lem-graph-as-pullback-diagonal","thm-classical-product-agrees-scheme-product","lem-base-change-surjective-morphisms","lem-immersions-and-localizations-monomorphisms","rem-properties-can-change-after-field-extension","rem-fibre-is-scheme-not-point-set","ex-fibre-family-xy-equals-t","ex-nonreduced-fibre-x-squared-equals-t","ex-base-change-real-conic-to-complex","cex-domain-tensor-algebraic-closure-reducible","ex-product-affine-lines-plane","ex-fibre-closed-immersion","ex-graph-polynomial-map-closed-subscheme","cex-set-theoretic-fibre-loses-multiplicity","ex-empty-fibre-tensor-zero-ring","cex-purely-inseparable-base-change-nonreduced","ex-quadratic-cover-self-fibre-product"],"conventions":[{"convention":"For Noetherian spaces, dimension is the supremum of lengths of chains of nonempty irreducible closed subsets; \u0000dim empty set equals minus infinity, including the empty supremum.","fixed_by":"def-dimension-noetherian-topological-space","matters_for":["lem-dimension-finite-union-components","def-dimension-classical-variety","def-fibre-dimension-at-point-classical","thm-upper-semicontinuity-fibre-dimension-projective","cex-dimension-empty-set-minus-infinity-needed"]},{"convention":"Classical varieties are over a fixed algebraically closed field under Choice; irreducible means nonempty, and the dimension-page fibres and points are reduced closed-point classical fibres and points unless stated otherwise.","fixed_by":"lem-classical-variety-noetherian-components","matters_for":["thm-dimension-equals-transcendence-degree","thm-generic-fibre-dimension","thm-upper-semicontinuity-fibre-dimension-projective","ex-fibre-dimension-jump","rem-equidimensionality-hypotheses"]},{"convention":"A scheme-theoretic fibre at any point s is X times over S Spec kappa(s), without reduction; nonclosed and generic points and empty fibres are allowed.","fixed_by":"def-scheme-theoretic-fibre","matters_for":["thm-affine-fibre-coordinate-ring","lem-points-of-fibre-primes-over-point","lem-scheme-fibre-stalk-quotient","lem-fibre-after-base-change","ex-nonreduced-fibre-x-squared-equals-t","cex-set-theoretic-fibre-loses-multiplicity"]},{"convention":"Geometric fibre properties are tested after base change to a chosen algebraic closure of kappa(s); the choice-independence assertion is noncanonical and uses Choice. Empty geometric fibres are reduced and connected, but neither irreducible nor integral.","fixed_by":"def-geometrically-reduced-integral-connected-fibre","matters_for":["lem-geometric-fibre-choice-independent","rem-properties-can-change-after-field-extension","cex-domain-tensor-algebraic-closure-reducible","cex-purely-inseparable-base-change-nonreduced"]}],"load_bearing":[{"id":"thm-fibre-products-of-schemes-exist","statement":"Every diagram X to S left-arrow Y of schemes has a fibre product, with affine chart cover Spec(B_ij tensor over A_i C_ik) for compatible affine charts.","used_by":["def-base-change-morphism-schemes","def-scheme-theoretic-fibre","lem-base-change-locally-finite-type-presentation","def-graph-morphism-over-base","thm-classical-product-agrees-scheme-product"]},{"id":"thm-affine-fibre-product-tensor-ring","statement":"For maps A to B and A to C of commutative unital rings, allowing the zero ring, Spec B times over Spec A Spec C is canonically Spec(B tensor over A C).","used_by":["thm-fibre-products-of-schemes-exist","thm-affine-fibre-coordinate-ring","lem-affine-fibre-product-projections-stalk-maps","ex-product-affine-lines-plane","ex-quadratic-cover-self-fibre-product"]},{"id":"thm-dimension-equals-transcendence-degree","statement":"If X is an irreducible classical variety, then dim X equals trdeg_k k(X), which is finite.","used_by":["thm-generic-fibre-dimension","cor-dimension-of-image-plus-generic-fibre","thm-dominant-equal-dimension-generically-finite","cor-dimension-affine-and-projective-space"]},{"id":"thm-generic-fibre-dimension","statement":"For a dominant morphism f:X to Y between irreducible classical varieties, there is a nonempty open U contained in f(X) such that every X_y for y in U is nonempty and has pure dimension dim X minus dim Y.","used_by":["cor-dimension-of-image-plus-generic-fibre","thm-dominant-equal-dimension-generically-finite","cor-closed-family-irreducible-equal-dimensional-fibres","rem-equidimensionality-hypotheses"]},{"id":"thm-chevalley-constructible-image-varieties","statement":"Every morphism of classical varieties sends every constructible subset to a constructible subset.","used_by":["cor-dominant-morphism-image-contains-open","ex-fibre-dimension-jump"]},{"id":"lem-points-of-fibre-primes-over-point","statement":"The projection X_s to X is a homeomorphism onto f inverse(s), preserves residue fields, and on affine charts identifies fibre points with primes contracting to the base point.","used_by":["lem-scheme-fibre-stalk-quotient","rem-fibre-is-scheme-not-point-set"]},{"id":"lem-points-of-scheme-fibre-product-residue-tensors","statement":"Points of X times over S Y correspond to (x,y,s,r) with common image s and r a prime of kappa(x) tensor over kappa(s) kappa(y), and have residue field kappa(r).","used_by":["lem-base-change-surjective-morphisms","rem-fibre-is-scheme-not-point-set","ex-product-affine-lines-plane"]},{"id":"thm-upper-semicontinuity-fibre-dimension-projective","statement":"For a projective morphism f:X to Y of classical varieties, E_r={y in Y: dim X_y at least r} is closed for every integer r.","used_by":["rem-equidimensionality-hypotheses"]}],"published_dependencies":[{"id":"def-noetherian-topological-space","used_for":"Noetherian-space convention used by the dimension definition and finite-cover arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"def-irreducible-topological-space-and-subset","used_for":"Nonempty irreducibility convention used in chain dimension and classical-variety arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"def-classical-algebraic-prevariety-regular-maps-and-varieties","used_for":"The classical variety, regular-map, separation, and finite-affine-cover framework for batch 9.","opened":true},{"id":"def-morphism-of-schemes","used_for":"The locally ringed-space meaning of scheme morphisms used in the fibre-product universal property.","opened":true}],"concerns":[],"alerts":[],"seams_checked":[]}